#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096354 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-405" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/f02616ea8f44865a727bec3affe596503dbadacafb113acad6e5f755218b4ff5.png)\n\nFor the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral." :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" :question/math-academy-id "e-406" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum." :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = 4-x,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(4-(1+\\frac{2k}{n}))\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(3-\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-407" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}$ and $f(x_k) = x_{k}^2.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)= x^2$ and $b = 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" :question/math-academy-id "e-408" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x$ as the limit of a right Riemann sum." :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = x^2,$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2218dc5-2872-527e-bfbf-42be9531102d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385744} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0955b2-e586-5b5c-b742-11399434e1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385745} {:answer/id #uuid "074ab172-dfad-5c73-8077-8e39d65394c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385746} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e08388-50b2-5b6d-95a2-97d4dd9eb538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385747} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad26cd3-df05-56eb-909e-9cc5e0efe275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2218dc5-2872-527e-bfbf-42be9531102d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385744} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1d932fb-161d-5b16-8d50-0c4323f3b00f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75ff0f89-478e-5f57-897a-97351f373753" :question/math-academy-id "q-113881" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97088cdb-cd2a-5a73-96c1-c7a102d98d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385750} {:answer/id #uuid "8abd8bd7-b559-5359-98e0-b19b3da7a962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385751} {:answer/id #uuid "0c31dd76-8a1b-5039-b468-fe9c431c293f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385752} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8cc40-575d-589b-8042-4992bb5df354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{2i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385753} {:answer/id #uuid "ed71c7f5-75e0-50d3-903a-eaaffb35caa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{2i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97088cdb-cd2a-5a73-96c1-c7a102d98d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385750} :answer-field/id #uuid "c86e92e6-2728-5090-930f-e82786eb7d08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ef22d3-07d6-525f-b1e0-653bfae49fd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113886" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4i}{n}-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fce448cc-8877-5522-b441-fc2e7683132a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385757} {:answer/id #uuid "b20db7ed-0ebc-52f7-b8b9-2cd94e8dd607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and $b=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385758} {:answer/id #uuid "b8049523-5f74-5db8-a4d8-5bca276db9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and $b=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385759} {:answer/id #uuid "e44fd72d-70e2-5290-a973-b6d30cb08334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385760} {:answer/id #uuid "98553cab-2b3d-57c1-bebf-33fe1975bd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $b=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98553cab-2b3d-57c1-bebf-33fe1975bd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $b=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385761} :answer-field/id #uuid "63ca64c2-8dbf-58db-ac83-188fd320e192" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4121c061-0943-55e1-8348-ce5b51dbf815" :question/math-academy-id "q-113917" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $b=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bb635d2-8d79-5ce9-b2ee-eb3d34326f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{6x}$ and $b=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385764} {:answer/id #uuid "d5661740-8911-5489-8656-11af23a612bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385765} {:answer/id #uuid "f9540f1c-dc4c-562f-995d-b19d8e72373d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{6x}$ and $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385766} {:answer/id #uuid "e939d2bc-48b6-5dd7-9f9b-bc0f3c9d5c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385767} {:answer/id #uuid "13795126-5909-5c2b-9dc5-2450f739e71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5661740-8911-5489-8656-11af23a612bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385765} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a81c35b-ade7-5ac5-a9bd-4f5f4f943d3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2081a2b-f662-517d-bd66-e0ddf60008b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113918" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{6}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=\\frac{6i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={e}^{{x}_{i}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7958e5c-0361-5d05-8dc2-93a2ab96f076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385770} {:answer/id #uuid "fa897aae-8b69-5d88-a33d-ba4d6d45acb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385771} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b9cffd-11fe-5a1d-a40d-3ca58ffa8af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385772} {:answer/id #uuid "2138eb5d-0e54-540f-8075-849885e89ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385773} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef1e332-4bad-5519-ab5a-6e7cd01579f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2138eb5d-0e54-540f-8075-849885e89ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385773} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc46cf96-20df-5762-b76e-74d771962837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1804c8ea-5802-59e5-92d9-206715562863" :question/math-academy-id "q-113927" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93c88a98-6d71-5a75-bdde-5a076f250275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{9+\\frac{4k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385776} {:answer/id #uuid "5da6d1aa-a178-5fed-8366-37801947f8b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385777} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6ffa08-4184-55f7-8eb9-616a3d7733f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385778} {:answer/id #uuid "a3809e43-c82c-51bf-9649-986ba0392fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385779} {:answer/id #uuid "f535ae35-0fd0-50c0-993e-9260a45c288e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b6ffa08-4184-55f7-8eb9-616a3d7733f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385778} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf3d76f-bba9-5196-9d85-816cbbecc4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23c94ac6-51bf-5545-8513-402f0a143fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113941" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a41e59a-844e-5ba7-9bfa-84e0c7acc2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{2}(x^{3} - 2x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095137} {:answer/id #uuid "d293c65e-1b66-5450-93e7-b85c8138a7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{1}^{1.5}(-x^{3} + 2x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095138} {:answer/id #uuid "e697f517-2f4c-5b29-a9c5-fa3dce350f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{1}^{0}(-x^{5} + x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095139} {:answer/id #uuid "eecfe745-8025-590d-9efd-b8cdd108e218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{1}(-x^{3} + 2x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095140} {:answer/id #uuid "555ca213-d2ad-5d55-a87d-a201c1fc24ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{2}^{0}(-x^{2} - 2x - 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eecfe745-8025-590d-9efd-b8cdd108e218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{1}(-x^{3} + 2x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095140} :answer-field/id #uuid "5944a245-4679-5c70-93ed-697eb82094b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd0dc7f3-b37b-5218-ae5c-70b1389fda98" :question/math-academy-id "q-30265" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/04e636bd831dbfd45ebed1bc8928c4b68d479a1147b98474c667b2aee7a5efc1.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f3beace-b065-547b-98f1-d8a6ca4ff881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-1.5}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385783} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2f8f2d-55c1-5e8e-be7e-baa5b9656513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=-{\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}({x}^{3}+x-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385784} {:answer/id #uuid "7f388d25-c2e5-50e1-8196-d0aadecf6d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{0.5}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385785} {:answer/id #uuid "e773d4ee-8d7f-5ea7-a7e4-d1b49c216804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385786} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd3d529-ead0-58d6-bf11-180c60790ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-0.5}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbd3d529-ead0-58d6-bf11-180c60790ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-0.5}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385787} :answer-field/id #uuid "519eb5c7-65f4-5829-b36d-d7b8babf2c73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5c06750-e5d4-53d0-b888-e83968e906f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-30271" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/b088ed9136d2b81e48752659369b24d2296924702a843629cca27d063c62b4df.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+x+2$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+x+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-0.5$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+x+2$ between the lower limit $x=-0.5$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-0.5}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80cede21-e3b5-501e-a9a9-d2e0c11bdda5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{3}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095143} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2d2966-63bc-55b8-b34a-b5bd972d5f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{3}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)^{2}\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095144} {:answer/id #uuid "357745ea-cdbe-5133-9e50-4d60041d798d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{-3}^{3}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095145} {:answer/id #uuid "40499ec4-d581-5cc6-bb6a-5eb07dd8b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{-2}^{2}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095146} {:answer/id #uuid "5f899f7e-eed6-5869-b959-35854e8974aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{-1}^{1}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f899f7e-eed6-5869-b959-35854e8974aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{-1}^{1}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095147} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bcf4f65-8908-5459-9f45-c028d98f3215" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08ba21a9-5bfb-51e6-9985-bff89e68f2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-30273" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/bb9db2eacfc01d00ca8a3436f9cd416afda7b400ae2aa2718d4ce049cd36a381.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ between the lower limit $x=-1$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ac202f-21a2-589b-b99f-b5ce58a3bb42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{1.5}^{3}(-{x}^{3}+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385790} {:answer/id #uuid "2de42af9-7007-52a0-853a-6cdd52fb9b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{0}^{-0.5}(-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385791} {:answer/id #uuid "bad4bf0d-9815-5ef7-be5d-6398024d2d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-1.5}^{0.5}(-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385792} {:answer/id #uuid "47eb10b0-38cc-5379-8838-281ff9a50ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{1.5}^{3}(-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385793} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0466a0-fcec-57cc-9b4d-85433c0a719e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-1.5}^{0.5}(-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bad4bf0d-9815-5ef7-be5d-6398024d2d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A={\\int }_{-1.5}^{0.5}(-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385792} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3781f8a-2fb0-5c7d-8619-e6de170820f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "034b151a-76c3-50b5-8c7c-2a70b048d143" :question/math-academy-id "q-30382" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/e8a9dfb3ff189e96089c73ead8a3b44f04aa1684894c44203afb8884ad75c9e1.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-1.5$ and $x=0.5.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2$ between the lower limit $x=-1.5$ and the upper limit $x=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1.5}^{0.5}(-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf514bb4-ecac-5d78-a01e-f1fa6b00f140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{3i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf514bb4-ecac-5d78-a01e-f1fa6b00f140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{3i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253122} :answer-field/id #uuid "82711246-7818-567e-acca-65c5baf0326d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ba8ef0d-4afc-57f3-a6e1-978f702b493c" :question/math-academy-id "q-315830" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=9{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9{(1+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83376cd6-9412-5702-a19a-8908538dbb62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83376cd6-9412-5702-a19a-8908538dbb62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385797} :answer-field/id #uuid "29450bb5-2f5e-5296-9d47-cdefdbb1cbf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385796} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07043285-b7be-5a87-80c3-0554ac463280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07043285-b7be-5a87-80c3-0554ac463280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385799} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47ad40b-156b-50b6-921e-6b7eddbf465e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d655b06f-d992-5719-bde5-20368aae480a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d655b06f-d992-5719-bde5-20368aae480a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385801} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c2d09c-04f7-58bd-9223-0960d06493a4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7149286b-e549-5d38-a39f-76c05cf90c8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346160" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/3b53e88e4f93985ca999c7fd9d4e3fe3591afe10e7fbe499a303d22bfb0ce6ea.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ between the lower limit $x=-3$ and the upper limit $x=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-3}^{2}\\sqrt{x+4}+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30f82af4-a1b4-5824-8336-f716548cdc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30f82af4-a1b4-5824-8336-f716548cdc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385804} :answer-field/id #uuid "56401737-9f0d-5ff7-af83-3bf5e5047657" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "958d273c-e6ff-5856-a66e-fef248a41117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958d273c-e6ff-5856-a66e-fef248a41117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385806} :answer-field/id #uuid "29474782-597b-53f8-90e6-756891c36b52" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48803847-d9e3-51f7-a9db-8dadbd254933" :question/math-academy-id "q-346179" :question/problem "Express ${\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}+2($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[({(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{3}+2(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)+1)\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d10c97e6-7ea4-5522-8feb-1d49481b5656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253125} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb83b21-9c07-50dc-ae3b-faa6b4be20c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb83b21-9c07-50dc-ae3b-faa6b4be20c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253126} :answer-field/id #uuid "f085c8ab-d5da-55c2-b14f-64548a810098" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aba5708e-7651-5cf7-ad88-1fb8cca73e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aba5708e-7651-5cf7-ad88-1fb8cca73e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253128} :answer-field/id #uuid "9749fad5-d5c5-57bf-af31-496187f79966" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "088d1080-7491-5e6a-87a2-5180d45dc341" :question/math-academy-id "q-346180" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={2}^{\\,{x}_{k}}+1.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}({2}^{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={2}^{x}+1\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76253847-5c1c-5932-9b74-c9030c3be821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385809} {:answer/id #uuid "f1449241-33e9-534c-b6e9-2764f7101594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76253847-5c1c-5932-9b74-c9030c3be821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385809} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3579a86-d0f6-5720-a22b-241fb1602c02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08283804-3f0c-567e-8092-893745fbb51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08283804-3f0c-567e-8092-893745fbb51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385812} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d181828-3d6d-5125-8de3-6d740bc6e541" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6467ec1c-4d5b-512b-882c-9fd6ebf57460" :question/math-academy-id "q-346204" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c5add23-77ea-56ab-9af4-ec8080c5169f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385814} {:answer/id #uuid "2b24346f-a740-5fc7-a5db-f84a119fbfd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385815} {:answer/id #uuid "1f594b39-7ae3-5662-9966-817c6bdc3ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{2l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385816} {:answer/id #uuid "75ca2f74-5289-5f31-acb2-dc8a2fe3e8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[-{(2+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385817} {:answer/id #uuid "b6af0b2b-6bbd-5fd0-a94f-8669a1c27289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{2l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b24346f-a740-5fc7-a5db-f84a119fbfd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385815} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a84a45-ea20-5014-a2ec-c2dffcda04ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ae0c14e-2353-57d2-951a-f4c503f5887d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49189" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+l\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{l}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f30f4f98-5972-58bb-9e57-181795cfd229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385821} {:answer/id #uuid "02c1dece-5c66-5afe-a533-5b49db99ee84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385822} {:answer/id #uuid "e9937bec-3f26-5e9b-a2ee-9fbc4176d202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385823} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa90623-787c-52b3-a2dd-9fc79760e879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385824} {:answer/id #uuid "b9213ea9-2606-52fc-9d8f-fdab15fe34b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(1+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02c1dece-5c66-5afe-a533-5b49db99ee84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385822} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c3d312-976d-5e5c-9edc-72a5cb0d7b96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c93ae6fa-f0fd-57d0-8eab-5d532d391d7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49192" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=5{x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=3$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "213a6db7-bdc3-5791-93c2-e0c917be5f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[\\frac{1}{16}{(\\frac{3l}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385828} {:answer/id #uuid "e89fda5f-83e1-5ce8-b94f-8c92a23a4e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{4l}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385829} {:answer/id #uuid "2320ec8b-89e7-5383-8e76-8646ad5ca6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[2{(4+\\frac{l}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385830} {:answer/id #uuid "68101627-b636-5f0a-a329-e3f67957dd2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[16{(\\frac{7l}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385831} {:answer/id #uuid "befe9551-7bbe-5e79-ad42-52f87c9b7f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[2{(1+\\frac{3l}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "befe9551-7bbe-5e79-ad42-52f87c9b7f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[2{(1+\\frac{3l}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385832} :answer-field/id #uuid "4461689f-aeae-56fa-9d76-4ce561b0483b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28b01a23-9ced-5e00-9c4d-68c57434b2da" :question/math-academy-id "q-73159" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{4}2{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}2{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=2{x}^{-4},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-(1)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}2{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+l\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{3l}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[2{(1+\\frac{3l}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6e8f3d3-ecf1-5080-ab3e-5ab0c4796c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385834} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe4a753-e2e6-5f02-8410-682e51daaae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385835} {:answer/id #uuid "48e88ef6-36b5-5f68-aaf2-889636a8f998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385836} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2e4ad0-d60e-5be6-83bd-b06c8491188f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385837} {:answer/id #uuid "85649e47-8569-5ade-bb69-b89a734da890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85649e47-8569-5ade-bb69-b89a734da890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a07355-2af6-536b-8287-343dce50070a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc6ae514-8e76-5ad4-a9b5-b333e0e65f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73178" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc999a6-0cae-5013-8181-8ade8e22b109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385841} {:answer/id #uuid "cf607ef3-8bba-5406-b207-b748c3ea3a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385842} {:answer/id #uuid "70c723d0-7cd6-556a-8777-0917dedfb921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[2\\sqrt{6+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385843} {:answer/id #uuid "8f04aae0-f14a-5335-aed2-582cb2f8c771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385844} {:answer/id #uuid "c4923bd4-2df6-587a-8263-fafc6b925ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebc999a6-0cae-5013-8181-8ade8e22b109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385841} :answer-field/id #uuid "a080a941-b22b-54d0-acd8-c8325ea1d73e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d76772d8-d5b1-522d-b84a-c52ac6da6060" :question/math-academy-id "q-73182" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-2}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
